# Source documents are EVIDENCE. They are identified by SHA-256 in
# policies/sources.yaml, and the extractors refuse to run on a hash mismatch.
#
# With core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default, and set in this repo), git rewrites
# line endings on checkout. The blob stored in git stays correct, but the file on
# disk does not — the calendar HTML committed as LF materialises as CRLF in a fresh
# clone, its hash changes, and extract_calendar.py stops with a mismatch. The PDF
# escapes this only because git auto-detects it as binary.
#
# Caught by validating from a clean worktree rather than the one the files were
# authored in. Marking these paths binary tells git to leave the bytes alone, which
# is the only way a content hash can mean anything across machines.
policies/sources/** -text -diff

# Generated from the PDF text layer; their SHA-256s are recorded in INDEX.json, so
# they are subject to exactly the same corruption.
policies/evidence/page_text/** -text
